"""Long-form coaching sections. Shared role lessons plus explicit champion decisions.

No inferred kit mechanics: specific claims stay in reviewed notes/decisions.
Role guidance is conditional, not a claim that every champion has every tool.
"""
from pathlib import Path

# Each pattern has six distinct teaching sections. These explain transferable
# decisions; the champion's kit plan and worked decision make the application explicit.
DEPTH = {
'assassin': [
('Early game: earn a safe opening', '''Build a position from which a short exchange does not cost your entire health bar or leave you trapped. Watch what the opponent uses to clear the wave or contest a nearby resource. That action can create an opening, but first check whether their partner or jungler can answer. If you are laning, account for the minions you leave when moving toward a side route. If you are jungling, compare a speculative chase with the safe camp or cover opportunity you give up. Pressure is useful when the opponent must respect it; you do not need to spend your full sequence every time they enter reach.'''),
('Midgame: create the pick before the fight', '''Move after useful information, not merely after the first enemy portrait appears. A target collecting a wave is a possible pick only if you know enough about nearby defenders and can return after the attempt. Approach with an ally or through checked cover while the rest of the team has something useful to do. If nobody can use the pressure, a long flank may leave your own formation outnumbered. When the target retreats early, take the space you earned and regroup. Do not reveal from the same route repeatedly and expect the enemy to stop preparing for it.'''),
('Read the enemy formation', '''Against grouped protection, wait for the defender to spend attention or an important response on someone else. Against a separated target, verify that the separation is real rather than a short gap in vision. Against a team that engages onto you, a defensive finish beside your own carry may be the best opening. These are different jobs: assassination, edge pressure and counterattack. Choose the one the current formation permits. If the enemy keeps a defensive tool unused because you are nearby, that restraint can help your teammates. Do not throw away that pressure simply because you have not yet dealt damage.'''),
('Run a complete entry and exit sequence', '''Before moving in, name the first action, the enemy response you expect and the location of your next safe action. During entry, watch the response rather than staring only at the target's health bar. A shield, displacement or newly arriving defender can change the value of the continuation. After using your initial damage, decide whether you can keep contact without losing the exit. Leave when the next safe damage window is worse than returning to allies. A surviving target does not automatically mean the attempt failed: forcing a retreat or an important defensive spell can create the next team advantage.'''),
('Change the plan when the fight turns', '''When ahead, use the threat of a fast kill to restrict enemy routes, but keep checking whether the opponent has gathered enough protection to punish you. When behind, reduce the demand you place on your own damage: help finish a target already pressured by allies or keep a vulnerable enemy from freely entering. If the first approach is seen, change the timing or route instead of speeding up the same failed entry. If your team retreats, retreat too unless a specific safe action can change the situation. Waiting at a flank while teammates die is not the same as preserving a useful threat.'''),
('Convert pressure into something the team keeps', '''After a successful pick, identify the safe wave, structure or objective your team can actually reach. Return toward that shared action instead of pursuing a second target into unknown ground. If the target escapes, use the information gained to decide whether another attempt is plausible before your resources recover. In a replay, compare the value of your first entry with the value of your next movement. Many good picks become poor outcomes because the player keeps chasing after the useful fight has ended. A clean exit leaves you available to threaten the next approach and helps your team retain its numbers advantage.''')],
'marksman': [
('Early game: protect repeatable attacks', '''Use the early exchanges to learn which enemy action prevents you from attacking safely. It may be direct control, a flank route or pressure from a second opponent. Take attacks when that response is unavailable or when your partner can cover it, then move before the window closes. If you are farming a lane, do not trade away all your health merely to contest one minion. If your champion is used elsewhere, apply the same rule to the resource you are collecting. The goal is a repeatable pattern of damage and income, not a single impressive exchange that leaves you unable to stay on the map.'''),
('Midgame: move with a reason and protection', '''Before moving toward a fight, identify the route that gives you a safe first attack position. Arriving early through an unchecked path can be worse than arriving slightly later with support. Catch available resources when the team does not need immediate presence, but account for how long it takes to return to the next contest. Avoid following a distant enemy while allies are resetting. If the team cannot protect a forward position, take a safer wave or wait in controlled space. Continued development matters because your role usually needs several attack opportunities, and a preventable death removes all of them for a time.'''),
('Read the enemy access tools', '''Separate the enemies you want to damage from the enemies who can stop you from dealing damage. The most urgent threat may be a durable diver, not the opposing carry. Against long-range pressure, move between casts and avoid standing in the same line as every teammate. Against flank access, hold a route back to an ally and check the edge before focusing on the frontline. Against a protected formation, accept safe damage on the nearest target until the formation changes. Your target can change during the fight; the rule is to choose the best damage available from a position that survives the relevant response.'''),
('Manage the first attack window', '''Start from the safe edge and test whether the enemy is actually committing. Attack or cast, move with the formation, then check the next threat before repeating. Do not spend a defensive movement tool just because it adds a small amount of damage. If your kit has a special firing window, prepare the position before activating it so travel does not consume the benefit. If an ally moves away, your safe area may shrink immediately even when no enemy has moved. Stop attacking briefly when needed to rebuild the position. That pause can preserve many more attacks than standing still through the next engage.'''),
('Adapt when ahead, behind or under pressure', '''When ahead, keep dealing damage through protection rather than proving the lead with an unsupported duel. Enemies may need to commit more tools to reach you, which makes patience even more valuable. When behind, reduce unnecessary exposure and contribute to a controlled target; a lower damage window is still better than no window. When your protection disappears, move toward the nearest controlled route and accept that the fight may have ended for you. Do not cross the enemy line to reach a teammate who cannot actually help. Recovery begins by keeping the next useful farming and fighting opportunity available.'''),
('After a takedown: advance one safe step', '''A falling opponent changes the formation but does not reveal every remaining threat. Check the survivor with the best access before moving forward to clean up. If a reset or movement reward is part of your kit, use it to reach another safe position rather than assuming it grants immunity. Help the team convert the fight through a reachable objective or wave, then reset when health and resources no longer support contact. Review the first forward step after each takedown. That is often the moment where a strong attack pattern turns into a preventable death and gives away the objective the team had just earned.''')],
'bruiser': [
('Early game: choose the length of the exchange', '''Learn what lets you maintain contact and what forces you to leave. A short exchange is useful when your first actions are strong but the enemy response would win a longer fight. A sustained exchange needs a reachable target, resources and enough room to keep moving. Check the surrounding units and nearby enemies before turning contact into a chase. In lane, a large hostile wave can change the result; in the jungle, a second opponent can arrive from an unchecked route. End the exchange when its conditions disappear. Continuing merely because the fight has already begun is not a plan.'''),
('Midgame: connect side pressure and team presence', '''Decide whether the next useful action is a safe side wave, a nearby skirmish or a grouped contest. Do not choose side pressure without accounting for missing enemies and the time needed to join allies. If the team is already being approached, arriving through a supportable route may be more useful than taking one more resource. If no contest is imminent, gather what you can safely use and prepare the next movement. Your melee presence has value when allies can benefit from the space it creates. Walking ahead of the formation without a specific reason can give opponents a free focus target.'''),
('Identify the reachable fight', '''Against a ranged team, look for cover, allied control or a constrained approach that reduces the distance you must cross. Against other melee threats, ask which opponent can be held near your team long enough for repeated damage. Against a protected carry, do not assume the correct job is always to dive. Stopping the enemy diver may provide the same team advantage with much less travel. Choose an accessible first target and update that choice when movement changes the formation. A valuable target is one your kit and team can affect now, not simply the enemy with the largest damage total.'''),
('Use the defensive window while it matters', '''Plan protection around the response you expect after contact. Different champions use different forms of durability, recovery or avoidance, so follow the specific kit plan above rather than copying another champion's timing. Enter when the defensive window and allied pressure overlap. While it lasts, maintain useful contact instead of spending the whole period chasing. When it ends, move back toward support unless another safe sequence is available. Recovery that depends on hitting something does not help when the target leaves reach. A defensive tool also does not replace an exit from a fight where every enemy can continue attacking you.'''),
('Switch between pressure and protection', '''If the enemy carry remains unreachable, turn toward the threat your teammates can damage. Use body position and any available control to limit that enemy's access while continuing your own pressure. If allied control creates a clear opening, move forward together and reassess after the first response. When ahead, avoid taking damage from several enemies solely because you can survive the first few hits. When behind, choose shorter contact and clearer follow-up. A useful disruption followed by survival can contribute more than a long solo exchange that looks active but leaves your team without your next spell cycle.'''),
('Review the decision to stay', '''After every major action, ask whether the target is still reachable, allies can still help and your resources still support another exchange. If one answer changes, choose a new position before chasing. After a win, use your presence to secure safe space for the team's objective rather than beginning another isolated fight. In review, pause when your main defensive window ends and inspect the next movement. Compare leaving then with the continuation you chose. This reveals whether the problem was the original entry or the later decision to remain after the good part of the fight was already over.''')],
'duelist': [
('Early game: establish a repeatable trade', '''Use short exchanges to learn the opponent's response and your own exit route before demanding an extended duel. Prepare the resource, mark, stack or ability state described in the champion plan. Do not assume that having every button available means the setup is ready. Watch the nearby wave and missing enemies before extending. If you spend a movement or defensive action to begin, know what remains to answer retaliation. A good trade can end without a kill. Preserving enough health and position to contest the next resource is often more useful than forcing one more hit into the opponent's strongest response.'''),
('Midgame: make side pressure a team decision', '''Choose a side lane only when you can explain what the rest of your team gains while you are there. Check the wave's location, likely enemy answers, retreat routes and the next shared contest. If several enemies disappear, reduce the depth of the push until information returns. If allies cannot avoid a fight, decide whether your pressure can produce a real trade or whether you must join. Do not split simply because the champion is associated with dueling. The value comes from creating a difficult enemy decision while your team remains able to use the resulting space or numbers.'''),
('Choose a grouped role before arriving', '''In a teamfight, a clean isolated duel may not exist. Decide whether you will pressure a side angle, follow allied control or protect a teammate against the first diver. Against several ready disables, wait for the response that would stop your damage to be spent or occupied. Against enemies who spread, look for a target whose escape you can contest without leaving support entirely. Against a compact group, accept a reachable frontline exchange. Your best opponent in a side lane may not be the best first target in a crowded fight where every movement invites several additional responses.'''),
('Preserve one answer after the opening', '''Before beginning a sequence, identify the tool or route you will use when the opponent reacts. It may be movement, a timed defense, terrain or an allied position; use the actual kit rather than assuming a universal escape. Do not spend every option to reach a target who still holds their important response. Once contact begins, alternate damage with position checks. A precision mechanic only helps when the place required to execute it is survivable. Give up the perfect angle when reaching it would put you inside several enemies. The next safe exchange is usually worth more than completing one pattern at any cost.'''),
('Adjust the commitment to the game state', '''When ahead, make the opponent answer pressure without offering an easy collapse. The lead helps you win some exchanges, but it does not reveal missing enemies or remove control. When behind, gather safe resources and look for coordinated finishes or defensive contributions instead of repeating a lost isolated duel. If the target moves beyond the useful fight, stop and consider what the team needs next. Your ability to chase does not make every chase valuable. Re-enter only when the conditions that support the damage pattern return, rather than relying on the same sequence to produce a different result against stronger protection.'''),
('End the duel and choose the next job', '''After a favorable exchange, decide between a safe structure, a wave, a reset and joining allies. Check the map before treating a single opponent's retreat as permission to advance indefinitely. In a grouped fight, move back into shared space when the first target leaves useful range. Review the moment when your available exit became unavailable: did you spend it to begin, use it for minor damage or lose it by walking too far? That distinction gives a concrete practice goal. Fix the earliest decision that removed choice, rather than focusing only on the final hit that killed you.''')],
'engage': [
('Early game: threaten without overcommitting', '''Stand where an opponent must respect a possible start while keeping your ally within useful follow-up distance. A held control spell can deny a route or make a resource difficult to contest even when you never cast it. Watch how the opponent moves when your threat becomes available. If your partner is low, occupied or returning from base, adjust the position instead of playing as if full damage support exists. When moving between areas, check the route before spending your entry tool. Reaching an enemy is only the opening action; the exchange still needs a target, allied damage and a survivable continuation.'''),
('Midgame: prepare the place to fight', '''Help the team approach the next objective together. Use available information tools and allies to check entrances rather than assuming personal durability makes an unknown route safe. Choose the area where your damage dealers can stand and the path your control should keep enemies from using. If the team is late, look for a defensive catch or a safe trade elsewhere instead of forcing a blind entry into established enemy ground. Preparation reduces the distance between your first control and the follow-up. It also makes retreat possible when the enemy refuses the fight you wanted to start.'''),
('Choose the target by follow-up', '''Against a clustered team, several targets may be available but only some are reachable by allied damage. Against a mobile carry, the first action may need allied setup or a constrained escape route. Against a diving team, the most useful engage can be a counter-engage on the opponent who has already entered your formation. Do not measure success only by how many enemies are affected. A single important target held beside your team may decide more than a large group caught beyond its range. Before starting, identify the ally who will use the window and verify their position rather than assuming they will arrive.'''),
('Layer control and check the response', '''Use the first action to create a shared focus, then watch whether the enemy spends an escape, protection or counter-engage. Add the next control when it provides useful extra time or prevents a specific response; avoid overlapping every available effect without a reason. Once allies begin dealing damage, decide whether to stay on the target or turn toward the threat reaching your backline. Your job can change within the same fight. If the first engage misses, do not automatically spend the second tool chasing the original idea. Rebuild the formation and keep enough control to answer the enemy's attempt to punish the miss.'''),
('Distinguish durability from permission', '''Surviving the first hit does not mean a deep position is useful. Count the enemies who can continue attacking you and the allies who can punish them for doing so. When ahead, use control to secure a clear conversion rather than forcing a spectacular but unsupported dive. When behind, keep the entry shorter and make enemy approach expensive. Your control remains useful even if you cannot absorb a long response. If allies turn away, treat that as new information and protect their exit where possible. Do not rely on them reversing direction after you have already committed to an isolated target.'''),
('Review the setup, not just the hit', '''After the fight, help secure the next safe approach and let the team reset if resources are low. Do not begin a second engage because a cooldown has returned while allies are still recovering from the first. In review, pause immediately before commitment and measure the practical gap to allied damage. Then check whether a wall, wave or enemy threat blocked their route. A landed ability can coexist with a poor decision. The useful lesson is often to start later, choose a nearer target or stand where the same control also protects your team if the forward opportunity disappears.''')],
'protect': [
('Early game: make support range useful', '''Stay close enough to affect the next meaningful exchange without standing in the same exposed line as the ally you help. Identify which enemy action forces your teammate to stop attacking or retreat. Keep a response for that action rather than using every tool on harmless pressure. If you leave to ward or roam, first check whether the ally can safely hold their position and whether the route itself is controlled. Protection includes choosing when to be present. A spell that is ready but out of range cannot help, and following an ally into an unwinnable position may simply give the enemy two targets.'''),
('Midgame: choose who can use your help', '''Identify the ally who can contribute if kept alive and in position during the next fight. This may change with health, resources, available spells and the shape of the terrain. Do not follow the same teammate automatically through every situation. Move with the part of the formation that has a real next action, and check entrances with support rather than walking alone into unknown ground. If a teammate is isolated beyond rescue, protect the remaining group and look for a safe way to recover space. A successful save preserves a useful continuation; preventing one immediate death is not always enough.'''),
('Read the order of enemy threats', '''Against a single diver, preserve the tool that interrupts or weakens their decisive action. Against two threats, decide which one needs your strongest response and which can be handled through movement or allied help. Against ranged pressure, keep the team spread enough to avoid taking the same hit while remaining in support range. Against a team that retreats, do not spend every defensive tool chasing unless you have checked that counter-engage is unavailable. The visible enemy is not always the important one. Scan the route of the missing threat before focusing only on the health bar of the ally directly in front of you.'''),
('Time the response and move with it', '''Use the champion-specific protection at the point where it changes the outcome: before lethal damage when preparation is required, during a relevant approach when control can deny it, or as the ally needs a new position. Do not follow a fixed button order regardless of what the enemy does. After the response, move with the protected teammate so the next spell remains in range. If they retreat, help preserve that route; if they can continue safe damage, hold the formation. Watch the second attacker while the first is controlled. Spending everything at once can leave no answer when the enemy staggers its commitment.'''),
('When a save becomes too costly', '''Ask what position you and the ally will occupy after the save. If neither of you can leave and the team cannot follow, a deep rescue attempt may make the loss larger. Use the available range, control and terrain to improve the situation without blindly copying the ally's path. When ahead, preserve the damage source that can finish the useful fight rather than joining an unnecessary chase. When behind, help the team leave bad ground together and retain enough health for the next defense. Accept that protection has limits: an ally still needs a viable action after your tool has been spent.'''),
('Review whether support preserved action', '''After contact ends, escort vulnerable allies through checked routes and help establish the next safe position. Do not let a successful save turn into an immediate second face-check. In review, record what the protected ally could do after your intervention: attack, retreat, cast or merely delay the same unavoidable death. Then inspect your own position and the tool held for the next threat. This gives a more useful measure than raw healing or shielding totals alone. Good protection changes the team's options, and often the improvement is a small movement before the enemy commits rather than a dramatic reaction after it does.''')],
'control': [
('Early game: connect spell use with position', '''Use the early exchanges to learn where an opponent must move and which spell you need if they cross toward you. Do not spend the full kit on a low-value attempt when doing so leaves no answer to an approach. If you are managing a wave, compare the resources used to clear it with what remains for the next contest. If you are supporting or jungling, apply the same resource check to the next useful route. A spell that misses can still influence movement, but only if you retain a position from which the opponent cannot freely punish the cooldown.'''),
('Midgame: arrive before the space is lost', '''Choose the entrance or area the team wants to control before enemies settle into it. Move with allies so you can set pressure from protected ground rather than walking in as the first target. If the team arrives late, use safe information and ranged influence where available; do not assume a powerful zone lets you enter unknown terrain alone. Let the frontline or another supported ally create the first stable position. Advance your influence with them. A prepared area is valuable because opponents must pay a cost to cross it, not because you cast every spell the moment an objective appears.'''),
('Read movement before selecting the cast', '''Against a compact formation, place pressure across a route several enemies need. Against spread opponents, focus on the path of the immediate threat or a reachable target rather than chasing maximum area damage. Against a diver, reserve a response and enough space for it to work; delayed control may not stop access immediately. Against enemies who refuse your prepared ground, decide whether holding it already achieves the objective. You do not need to leave a good position simply because nobody walks into the obvious spell. Denying a route can help the team even when the damage numbers are small.'''),
('Think in two spell cycles', '''Plan where you will stand after the first cast sequence, not only where that sequence lands. Use allied setup or constrained movement to improve the chance of useful contact. While abilities recover, reposition behind support and watch the opponent who can bypass the zone. Avoid stepping forward for a small extra hit if it removes the room needed for your next full sequence. When the fight moves, move your influence with it instead of continuing to cast at the original location. A good first zone can become irrelevant once both teams change direction, so update the plan before spending the next important tool.'''),
('Use control differently when behind', '''When ahead, restrict the enemy's safe approaches and advance with allies after the relevant threats are checked. When behind, prioritize safe wave pressure, delayed approaches and a clear retreat route. Do not walk into unknown ground to manufacture a full damage sequence against a stronger formation. Let the enemy enter a position where control and allied focus can reduce its advantage. If your main defensive spell misses, give ground early instead of waiting until the opponent reaches you. Recovery depends on preserving another useful cast window, not proving that every lost area can be defended from the same standing position.'''),
('Convert a controlled area into a result', '''After forcing enemies away, check whether the team can safely use the space for a structure, objective, wave or reset. Preserve resources for the next contest rather than spending everything on an opponent already leaving useful range. In review, examine whether enemies had to cross your threatened area or could simply walk around it. Then inspect the position of your own body while the zone was active. A strong placement may still fail if you stand where an unrelated threat can reach you. Practise changing the angle or timing so both the area and the caster remain useful throughout the exchange.''')],
'poke': [
('Early game: build pressure you can keep', '''Look for a clear line when the opponent is occupied by movement, an attack or a resource. Cast from a place where you can move after the attempt rather than standing still to watch the result. If the spell misses, keep the defensive response needed for their approach. Balance resource use with the next wave or contest; repeated speculative casts can leave you unable to pressure when enemies finally commit. If your champion also uses attacks, take them only when the closer range is safe. The point of ranged pressure is to improve a later decision, not to land every possible small hit.'''),
('Midgame: establish the firing position first', '''Move toward the next contest with allies and identify both the firing line and the route away from a flank. Place pressure from controlled ground before the enemy reaches comfortable engage range. If your team arrives late, do not stand alone at an unchecked entrance merely because your spells reach through it. Wait for information or a supported route. Your range is useful only while the casting position survives. If opponents take cover, move with allies to create another angle rather than walking forward until the original range advantage has disappeared and the enemy can start a direct fight.'''),
('Read the enemy answer to pressure', '''Against a team that must approach through a narrow route, repeated ranged damage can make entry costly. Against strong engage, check who can cross the distance and keep a response for that champion. Against sustain or protection, ask whether the damage is actually changing the upcoming contest rather than assuming every hit creates a lasting advantage. Against a flank, turn attention to survival before finishing another distant cast. The enemy does not need to win the long-range exchange if it can reach your position directly. Track that access separately from the health bar of the opponent you are trying to weaken.'''),
('Turn a health lead into a good commitment', '''After useful damage lands, tell the difference between pressure and a confirmed opportunity. A lower-health enemy may still hold control, an escape and nearby protection. Let your team use the advantage by taking space or forcing an unfavorable approach rather than chasing the wounded target alone. When allies commit, cast along the movement they constrain and keep your own position behind the fight. If a channel is part of your kit, verify its location before starting and stop when the safety conditions change. A partial safe contribution can preserve another sequence where an uninterrupted but exposed attempt would end your fight.'''),
('Recover when the enemy closes', '''When the opponent reaches close range, stop following the distant damage plan. Use the available defensive tool, move toward support and establish a new line. If the team is behind, clear or pressure from the area it can actually defend instead of standing at the most aggressive possible range. If ahead, resist using that lead to face-check; unseen access can still remove the advantage. When a key defensive spell is unavailable, give the enemy less space to punish by retreating early. Treat movement between volleys as part of damage output because it is what makes the next volley possible.'''),
('Review the value of each firing line', '''After the enemy gives ground, move with allies and check the next approach before beginning another sequence. Convert the pressure into a reachable objective, wave or reset rather than spending all resources on a retreating target. In review, record where you stood before each meaningful hit and which threat could have reached that spot. Compare successful pressure with attempts that forced you to retreat immediately or die. The practice goal may be a better angle, a later cast or an earlier step backward. Accuracy matters, but the position that lets you repeat accurate casts often matters more than the longest possible shot.''')]
}

COMMON = [
('Build a personal matchup plan', '''Before the first meaningful exchange, identify three things about the opponent: how they begin contact, what makes their damage dangerous and how they leave. Compare those actions with your own kit plan instead of labeling the matchup simply good or bad. Pick one observable opening, such as an important response being used or an ally creating setup. Also name one condition that cancels the attempt, such as another enemy arriving or your escape route closing. Use the opening only while the conditions remain true. After a lost exchange, change one decision rather than assuming the entire matchup is impossible. This guide teaches that decision process; it does not provide a numerical matchup ranking or a fixed build for every patch.'''),
('A focused practice session', '''Begin in practice mode by checking the current tooltips and rehearsing the key sequence without an opponent. Add movement before trying to perform it quickly. Next, practise the same sequence with a deliberate stop: miss the setup, change the target or move away before the continuation. This teaches the choice to cancel rather than only the choice to commit. In a normal game, focus on one measurable behavior for three relevant situations. Record whether you checked the threat and exit before acting. A win does not automatically mean the behavior was correct, and a death does not automatically mean the opening was wrong. Keep the practice target narrow enough that you can describe the next change in one sentence.'''),
('Review without rewriting what you knew', '''Pause a replay just before your first committed move and use only the information visible to your team at that moment. List visible enemies, missing threats, nearby allies and the important resources you actually had. Predict the next action before continuing playback. Then compare the prediction with the response: was the error an inaccurate assumption, a missed mechanic or a decision to continue after conditions changed? Review one successful exchange as well as one failure so you can repeat the good setup. Do not use later knowledge of a hidden opponent to pretend their exact location was obvious. The useful improvement is a better check, safer route or clearer stopping condition for the next similar situation.'''),
('Check your understanding', '''Answer these questions before rereading the guide. What condition makes your champion's main strength useful? Which enemy response would make you delay the opening? What would you do if your intended target moved out of safe reach? Where should you stand when the first sequence ends? Compare your answers with the playstyle, weakness and worked decision sections. A useful answer names a target, a threat and a next position; “deal more damage” is too vague to guide an action. If your answer depends on an ability interaction you are unsure about, verify it in the current client before building a match plan around it. Repeat the exercise after practice and explain which decision you changed and why.''')]


def extended_sections(c, decision):
    """Keep section order stable and each paragraph unique within the guide."""
    sections = list(DEPTH[c['pattern']])
    sections.insert(2, ('Worked decision: when the first plan changes', 'Hypothetical practice situation. ' + decision))
    sections += COMMON
    return sections


# Second paragraphs for the three overview sections, by playstyle. They explain what the
# champion-specific note means in practice, so no overview section is a single sentence.
OVERVIEW = {
'assassin': dict(
 play='Assassins win by removing one important enemy quickly, then leaving before the rest of the team can respond. That makes timing more important than raw damage: the same combo that wins a fight after enemy crowd control is spent can lose it when used first. Spend most of a fight threatening, watching and positioning, and only a short moment committing.',
 strong='Use this strength to make the enemy play around you even when you are not attacking. A carry who must stand further back, or a support who must stay beside them, is already giving your team space. When you do enter, choose a target your damage can actually finish and keep part of your movement for the exit.',
 weak='Most assassin deaths come from entering too early or staying too long. Before you commit, name the enemy ability that would stop you; if it is still available, wait or pressure someone else. If the attempt fails, leave immediately rather than spending the rest of your cooldowns to rescue it.'),
'marksman': dict(
 play='Marksmen provide steady damage over the whole fight rather than one burst. Your value grows with every second you stay alive and attacking, so position is your most important stat. Think of each fight as a series of attacks from safe spots, with short moves in between, rather than one big moment.',
 strong='Turn this strength into an advantage by hitting whatever you can reach safely, usually the nearest enemy. Sustained damage on the frontline still wins fights because it forces enemies to commit early or retreat. Let your team create the openings; you make sure every opening costs the enemy health.',
 weak='Your main risk is being reached. Track the enemies who can dash, pull or flank onto you, and keep allies or distance between you and them. If one of them is missing, play further back until you know where they are. Surviving with less damage is better than dying with a little more.'),
'bruiser': dict(
 play='Bruisers are melee fighters who are durable enough to stay in contact but not so durable that they can ignore the enemy team. You win longer fights at close range, so your job is to choose fights where you can keep contact and to leave the ones where you cannot. Health, cooldowns and allied support all decide how long you can stay.',
 strong='Use this strength in extended fights near your team, where the enemy cannot easily walk away from you. Pressure the closest valuable target, and make the enemy carry decide between running from you or staying to fight. A bruiser who keeps contact for the whole fight often deals more damage than any single burst.',
 weak='The usual weakness is being kited or focused before reaching anyone useful. Approach from cover, with allies, or after key slows and crowd control are used. If enemies spread out or keep moving away, stop chasing and return to protect your team rather than following them alone.'),
'duelist': dict(
 play='Duelists are strongest in one-versus-one fights and often scale into dangerous side-lane threats. Your pressure makes the enemy send someone to stop you, which can give your team a numbers advantage elsewhere. In grouped fights you usually want an edge position where you can reach a target without being the first one hit.',
 strong='Use this strength by creating situations the enemy must answer: a side lane that needs a defender, or a flank that threatens their carry. Pick fights where you can isolate one target. When the enemy commits several players to stop you, ping it and make sure your team uses the time to take something.',
 weak='The weakness is getting caught alone or stopped by crowd control before you can fight. Push side lanes only when you know where the enemy team is, and leave before several enemies can reach you. In grouped fights, wait for the key crowd control to be used before entering.'),
'engage': dict(
 play='Engage champions start fights. You decide when and where the team fights by catching an enemy with crowd control or by entering the enemy formation. That power comes with responsibility: an engage is only good if your team can follow it. Before every start, check that your damage dealers are close and ready.',
 strong='Use this strength to choose fights your team wants: an enemy who walked forward alone, a carry without Flash, or a group standing in a narrow area. Even without casting, your threat makes enemies keep their distance. Holding that threat can be as useful as using it.',
 weak='The usual weakness is engaging when nobody can follow. Look back before you go in and count the allies who can reach the fight within a few seconds. If your engage misses, do not spend your remaining tools chasing; reposition and protect your team instead.'),
'protect': dict(
 play='Protective champions keep allies alive and stop enemies who try to reach them. Your value comes from the damage your teammates deal because you kept them safe. Stay close enough to help, but not so close that one enemy ability catches both of you.',
 strong='Use this strength by staying near the ally most likely to win the fight and saving your best tool for the enemy most likely to kill them. Shields, heals and crowd control are strongest at the moment an enemy commits. A well-timed save can turn a lost fight into a win.',
 weak='The usual weakness is spending your tools too early or on the wrong threat. Watch for the second attacker after the first one, and keep something for them. If an ally is too far ahead to save, protect the rest of the team rather than following them into a loss.'),
'control': dict(
 play='Control mages shape fights with area spells, slows and crowd control. You decide which spaces are safe for the enemy to walk through. Your damage is high when enemies have to stand in your zones, and much lower when they can walk around them.',
 strong='Use this strength before objectives and in narrow areas such as jungle entrances and river paths. Place spells where enemies must move, not only where they are standing now. Holding a zone that the enemy refuses to enter still helps your team, even if your spells deal little damage.',
 weak='The main weakness is being reached by divers while your spells are on cooldown. Keep one defensive spell or a clear exit for the enemy who can bypass your zone. Stand behind your frontline and move back during cooldowns rather than waiting where you cast.'),
'poke': dict(
 play='Poke champions deal damage from long range before a fight starts. A team that has taken heavy poke must either fight at a disadvantage or give up the area. Your goal is to make the enemy lose health and options without giving them a chance to reach you.',
 strong='Use this strength before objectives, sieges and planned fights. Damage that lands while the enemy waits for a dragon or defends a turret often decides whether they can contest at all. Keep landing safe hits, and tell your team when an enemy is low enough to force a fight.',
 weak='The usual weakness is being engaged on or flanked while you focus on distant targets. Watch enemy engage champions and keep a retreat route. If an enemy closes the distance, stop poking and reposition. Poke is useful only while you are alive to keep casting.'),
}
